# Framework Core Contract

This session designs and implements the core `Framework` assembly (`src/Framework/Framework`).

Full rules: `docs/Framework.md`. Read that file before adding a type to this assembly.

This contract applies **only** to core `Framework`. Do not use it as a dumping ground for `Framework.Infrastructure`, `Framework.Serialization`, `Framework.Security`, `Framework.Validation`, `Framework.Resilience`, `Framework.Observability`, `Framework.Messaging`, or `Framework.Web`.

## Session protocol

- Do not start implementation or invent types until the user explicitly asks.
- When the user gives a task, do only that task.
- Preserve existing naming: `Framework.Time.ISystemClock`. Implementation stays in `Framework.Infrastructure.Time.SystemClock`.
- Do not add `IClock`, `IDateTimeProvider`, or a second clock abstraction in Common/Application.
- Do not invent `Result`, `Error`, `IIdGenerator`, or Framework exceptions to populate folders.
- Never silently move Domain exceptions or sibling Framework types into this assembly. Propose first.
- Do not create empty `Abstractions` / `Results` / `Errors` / `Exceptions` folders.

## Responsibility

`Framework` is the lowest-level, technology-independent surface. It provides reusable mechanisms, not business behavior.

It must not contain business logic or application-specific infrastructure.

## MUST NOT depend on

`Common.*`, `Stora.*`, other `Framework.*` assemblies, EF Core, ASP.NET Core, Redis, brokers, Keycloak, Serilog, OpenTelemetry, FluentValidation, Polly.

Keep `Framework.csproj` free of package and project references unless a justified BCL-only need is documented in the same change.

Other Framework assemblies may depend on `Framework`. Domain must not depend on `Framework`.

## MUST NOT contain

Domain entities/aggregates/events, application commands/queries/handlers, business validators, repositories, DbContext, Redis/broker/identity clients, ASP.NET middleware/controllers, logging/OTel/Polly implementations, HTTP/API models.

## Allowed (only when requested and justified)

Generic abstractions, primitives, Result/Error types, technical Framework exceptions, generic extensions/constants.

Existing public API: `ISystemClock`. Treat every new public type as a stable contract. Default implementations to `internal`. One type per file. Namespace = `Framework.{Folder}`.

## Clocks

| Contract | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| `ISystemClock` | `Framework` (implementation: `Framework.Infrastructure.Time.SystemClock`) |

There is a single clock abstraction for the whole solution. Application and Infrastructure inject `ISystemClock`. Do not introduce `IClock` or adapters that wrap the same concept.

## Result and exceptions (if added later)

Result types stay generic: no HTTP status codes, no API models, no `Result.NotFound("Order...")`.

Framework exceptions are technical only. `OrderNotFoundException` and similar belong to Domain (`DomainException`).

## Admission test

Before adding a type, all must be yes: generic, reusable, technology-independent, free of business concepts, and naturally owned by **core** `Framework`.
